=== p2-windows · W7 · page:settings/Settings (WinUI 3 / Fluent) ===
Parity unit : page:settings/Settings   kind=page   web route=/settings
Web source  : web/src/features/settings/pages/SettingsPage.tsx
ADR refs    : ADR-002, ADR-004, ADR-005, ADR-006, ADR-011, ADR-013, ADR-014, ADR-015
Runner      : .NET 10.0.300 SDK present (dotnet --version 10.0.300) — NOT blocked.

=== APPROACH ===
- The repository's WinUI app is code-first C# (no .xaml page files); every feature surface lives under
  apps/windows/TeslaSync.App/feature-views/<Name>/ as <Name>.Model.cs (pure, Microsoft.UI-free projection,
  unit-tested headlessly), <Name>.Source.cs (the generated-client feed), <Name>.ViewModel.cs
  (INotifyPropertyChanged + IDisposable state holder) and <Name>.cs (the Fluent view), exactly as the sibling W7
  ExportsPage / OnboardingPage do. The generated prompt's idealized "SettingsPage.xaml + SettingsPageViewModel.cs
  under TeslaSync/Features/Settings" is mapped onto these real conventions (namespace TeslaSync.App.FeatureViews.Settings).
- Pre-existing build blocker (FIXED, tightly coupled to the registration seam I had to edit): dotnet build of the
  solution was red BEFORE any change — the WinUI XAML markup compiler crashed with WMC9999 ("Object reference not set")
  which cascaded into spurious "unknown type" errors for every shared component. Root cause: a malformed code-behind in
  the exact Register(...) block I extend — ShellWindow.xaml.cs Register("Locations", () => { … }) was never closed
  (missing `return page; });`), so the next Register nested inside it and the constructor's brace balance broke. The
  XAML compiler chokes parsing the broken partial class. Closing the Locations lambda greens dotnet build (0/0) and is a
  prerequisite for adding the Settings registration to a file that compiles.
- Scope (manifest is the source of truth): apps/parity/parity-manifest.json scopes page:settings/Settings to exactly 17
  items — 3 panels (GlassPanel1/2/3, note "component: GlassPanel"), 2 states (loading, success) and 12 strings. The web
  page also mounts four named settings SECTIONS (<GeneralSettings/>, <AppearanceSettings/>, <AdvancedSettings/>,
  <ResetSection/>); these are their OWN parity units (separate component files already in-repo) and require an
  ISettingsRepository + generated IApiClient with no empty/in-memory source, exactly as the sibling pages deliberately
  avoid wiring a live client. They are out of this page's scope. The page reproduces every region it OWNS: the
  PageContainer chrome, the SettingsSearch box, the EditConflictBanner, and the three glass panels.
- Navigation entry (ADR / W4 seam): route "Settings" (RouteTable.cs path `settings`) already exists. Registration
  (ShellWindow.xaml.cs) is a factory: new FeatureViews.Settings.SettingsPage() whose NavigationRequested -> NavigateTo
  (the cross-page search deep-links + the Data Export panel -> "data-export", a real RouteTable route) and
  TourLauncherRequested -> NavigateTo("onboarding") (web dispatchTourLauncherOpen -> re-run the guided walkthrough).
- Data binding (ADR-004): the manifest data source is useSettings (GET /settings). The view-model reads through an
  injected ISettingsFeed; the default EmptySettingsFeed resolves the empty snapshot (the host-injection precedent the
  sibling W7 pages set), and the generated-client-backed SettingsClientFeed (SettingsPage.Source.cs) binds the generated
  OpenAPI contract client operation get_api_v1_settings (Operations.Settings.Get). No HTTP touches the view; the read
  JSON round-trips through the tolerant SettingsSnapshot.FromJson (accepts the bare object + the platform {data:…} envelope).
- Two data states (manifest: loading -> success): the web page consumes useSettings SOLELY for the PageContainer
  `loading` prop and then renders its (static) content for every resolved value. The native port reproduces exactly these
  two states per ADR-006: loading -> PageContainer.IsLoading spinner; success -> the SettingsSearch + EditConflictBanner
  + the three FadeIn glass panels. The PageContainer body is never blank. A feed exception is caught in
  SettingsPageViewModel.LoadAsync and resolves to success (the web pessimistic gate — content always renders after the
  initial spinner), asserted by ViewModel_transitions_from_loading_to_success_after_load.
- Panels: GlassPanel1 (Data Export) is the whole-card deep-link the web renders as <a href="/data-export"> — reproduced
  as a transparent Button wrapping a TsGlassPanel so it carries link keyboard+pointer+Narrator semantics; GlassPanel2
  (Onboarding Tour) hosts a Subtle TsButton "Open Tour Launcher"; GlassPanel3 (Setup Checklist) hosts a Subtle TsButton
  "Restart Checklist" and surfaces the web success toast (checklist.settings.restarted) via an InfoBar on restart.
  Each panel = BuildIconBox (tokenized accent FontIcon, ADR-005) + PanelTitle + Caption + trailing affordance.
- Live freshness / polling (ADR-013): N/A — settings is a one-shot REST read with no SSE/live signal source and no
  refetch interval on the web page; no >2-min staleness pill applies.
- SI units (ADR / P1-S5): N/A — this surface bears no unit values (it is preference chrome + static link/tour/checklist
  cards); the unit-bearing selectors live in the separate GeneralSettings unit.
- i18n (ADR-014): all 12 keys are present verbatim (web key names) in Strings/en/Resources.resw as translation.* with the
  web English values; the surface resolves them through the ILocalizer facade with the web key names + English defaults
  (PassthroughLocalizer in tests records every key). The flat translation.* catalog shares the short keys title/subtitle
  across pages, so the SI converters' display boundary uses the verbatim web English defaults; the ar/he catalogs lag en
  by design and fall back to the English default via ILocalizer until translated.
- a11y (ADR-015): the PageContainer carries the page heading-level-1 landmark so the UserControl wrapper is
  AccessibilityView.Raw; the Data Export card is a Button (link role) with AutomationProperties.Name + ToolTip; the tour
  and checklist actions carry AutomationProperties.Name; decorative icon boxes / the external-link glyph are
  AccessibilityView.Raw; the page exposes a Group automation peer; design tokens drive light/dark.

